        "text": "Array initialization with curly braces",
        "refs": ["Q0001"]
      },
      "content": {
        "text": "A newer way to initialize an array in Java is to omit the new operator and use curly braces with the items listed inside, e.g., int[] numbers = {2, 3, 5, 1, 4};",
        "refs": ["Q0001"]
      },
      "aliases": null
    }
  ],
  "definitions": [],
  "explanations": [],
  "examples": [],
  "facts": [
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "In Java, integer array elements default to 0.",
        "refs": ["Q0000"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "In Java, boolean array elements default to false.",
        "refs": ["Q0000"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "In Java, String array elements default to an empty string.",
        "refs": ["Q0000"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "Java arrays have a fixed size; once created, items cannot be added or removed.",
        "refs": ["Q0002", "Q0003"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "The length of a Java array can be read using the .length field.",
        "refs": ["Q0002"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "Java arrays can be sorted using the Arrays.sort method, which is overloaded for different parameter types.",
        "refs": ["Q0003", "Q0004"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    },
    {
      "fact_text": {
        "text": "Multi‑dimensional arrays can be created in Java, such as two‑dimensional arrays for matrices and three‑dimensional arrays for cube data.",
        "refs": ["Q0004"]
      },
      "fact_type": null
    }
